# Who Wired This - Local Multiplayer and ScriptableObject Rules

## Local Multiplayer Consistency
- Treat Player A and Player B as a paired contract; avoid one-sided behavior unless explicitly required.
- Keep control bindings/data assets mirrored across players when introducing new actions.
- For split/dual viewport logic, preserve compatibility with existing viewport switching systems.
- If a change touches one player prefab variant, evaluate whether the other variant requires equivalent updates.

## Player Prefab Variant Policy
- Prefer editing shared/base behavior first, then apply minimal variant-specific overrides.
- Do not duplicate logic across player variant scripts when one shared script can own behavior.
- Keep variant prefab changes explicit and intentional; avoid accidental overrides from broad inspector edits.
- Preserve existing naming and folder conventions under `Assets/WhoWiredThis/Prefabs/Players` and `Assets/FirstPerson/Prefabs`.

## ScriptableObject-First Configuration
- Gameplay tuning and station/module setup data must live in ScriptableObject assets where the project already follows that pattern.
- Add new serialized config fields to ScriptableObjects before hardcoding constants in gameplay scripts.
- Keep runtime systems consuming ScriptableObject data through explicit serialized references.
- If adding new data assets, place them in existing domain folders (`Data/...`) and keep naming deterministic by role/player.

## Integration Guardrails
- Reuse existing adapters/configurators for tutorial and first-person integration before introducing new entry points.
- Keep input, player action routing, and tutorial station configuration contracts aligned across `WhoWiredThis` and `FirstPerson`.
- If a requested change breaks parity or data ownership, call it out and request confirmation before proceeding.
